stood t=E1=BA=A1i sao m=E1=BB=8Di codebase t=C3=B4i t=E1=BB=ABng work on ev=
entually became "unmaintainable monsters."</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Ousterhout identified 2 core cau=
ses c=E1=BB=A7a software complexity:</p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Dependency</strong>: Kh=
i code kh=C3=B4ng th=E1=BB=83 understood ho=E1=BA=B7c modified in isolation=
</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Obscurity</strong>: Khi=
 important information kh=C3=B4ng obvious</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Historical Context:</str=
ong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Ousterhout's book (2018) came t=
=E1=BB=AB decades c=E1=BB=A7a experience teaching software design t=E1=BA=
=A1i Stanford. Unlike academic theories, n=C3=B3 based tr=C3=AAn practical =
observations v=E1=BB=81 t=E1=BA=A1i sao student code becomes complex.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Key insight: <strong>Complexity =
l=C3=A0 incremental</strong> - n=C3=B3 accumulates t=E1=BB=AB thousands of =
small decisions, kh=C3=B4ng ph=E1=BA=A3i t=E1=BB=AB single big architectura=
l mistakes.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Alternative Solutions v=
=C3=A0 Trade-offs:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Previous approaches focused on:<=
/p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Modularity</strong>: Br=
eaking down big problems</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Abstraction</strong>: H=
iding implementation details</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Design Patterns</strong=
>: Reusable solutions</li>
</ul>
